
  - My (a) "training-prep keeps authoring the initial range" was wrong — it doesn't author anything. The question is really about the microcycle
  note.
  - You asked whether per-mesocycle range changes would play well with this. They're already how it works. 15–20 isn't stale data — it's your
  current stated intent for this block. A retune to 8–12 in the store would silently contradict the note you're still maintaining, and next
  mesocycle you'd write 15–20 again and it would never take effect. That's the mirror image of today's bug: instead of a range nothing can
  change, a range your authoring can't change.

  Revised options — who owns the range:

  - (a) Store wins; retire the microcycle's rep-range column. The retune owns it outright, the agent stops sending. Clean single owner, but you
  lose the ability to deliberately set a range per mesocycle — the thing you told me you want.
  - (b) Microcycle authors, store adapts within the block. (Recommended.) A posted range that differs from the last one posted for that exercise
  is a deliberate re-authoring and overwrites the stored range; a posted range identical to the last is a no-op, so the nightly repost never
  clobbers a retune. New exercises get their range on first sight for free. You keep per-mesocycle intent, the retune adapts inside it, and
  neither silently beats the other.
  - (c) The retune writes back into the microcycle note. Truest to "one source", but it means strength-log editing a vault file the training
  agent owns — a new cross-repo write path, and ADR 0039 spent real effort keeping that surface small.

  I'd take (b). It's a change to ticket 02's scope — from "stop sending" to "adopt on change" — and it's implementable entirely inside
  strength-log, so personal-agents needs no change at all.

  Worth noting under (b): accepting a retune to 8–12 while the note still says 15–20 leaves them disagreeing until you next edit the microcycle.
  That's tolerable — the note is intent, the store is what the app runs on — but if that bothers you, (c) is the only option that fixes it.

  Slice 03 is unaffected and still running.

